---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DomainExtensionLookup from '@/components/tools/reference/DomainExtensionLookup.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="domain-extension-lookup">
  <DomainExtensionLookup client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What the domain extension lookup shows</h2>
    <p>
      Type a hostname, a URL or an email address. The domain extension lookup splits the hostname into
      the subdomain, the registrable domain and the public suffix, and shows the top-level domain (TLD)
      with its type and the organisation that manages it in the root zone.
    </p>
    <h2>Public suffixes and registrable domains</h2>
    <p>
      A public suffix is a part of a name under which anyone can register: <code>com</code>,
      <code>co.uk</code> or <code>github.io</code>. The registrable domain is the public suffix plus one
      label. Browsers use the same Public Suffix List to decide which pages are the same site for
      cookies and storage, and email systems use it to find the organisational domain for DMARC.
    </p>
    <p>
      The list has an ICANN part (suffixes run by registries) and a private part (suffixes that
      companies open to their customers). Switch off private suffixes to see the result with the ICANN
      part only.
    </p>
    <h2>All top-level domains</h2>
    <p>
      The list below has every TLD in the IANA root zone. Each square in the field is one TLD, ordered
      by type; the squares that match your search fill in. Internationalised TLDs show in their own
      script, with the ASCII form that DNS uses.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
